msgid "Place in the framework"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*EMStudio among the other tools of the Extended Matrix Framework: the "
"library, the node, EM Tools, Heriverse.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio is the editor of the Extended Matrix Framework, and it works "
"together with the other tools of the framework rather than in their "
"place. Each tool does one part of the work, and all of them read and "
"write the same study."
msgstr ""

msgid "The shared library: s3Dgraphy"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**s3Dgraphy** is the reference implementation of the EM language: the "
"definition of every kind of node and relation, the rules that say which "
"relation may join which nodes, the reading and writing of ``em.json``, "
"the projection to RDF. EMStudio, EM Tools, the StratiGraph node and "
"StratiField all use it, so they speak the same EM. A change to the "
"language is made once, in the library, and reaches every tool."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio uses the library in two ways. The window you work in carries a "
"copy of the library's definitions, so that it can draw the matrix and "
"check a relation at once. The heavier work (the RDF projection, the "
"reading of a table through a mapping, the chronology, the files on disk) "
"goes to EMStudio's **bridge**, a small local service that runs the "
"library itself. When a command needs the bridge and the bridge does not "
"answer, EMStudio says so in the window concerned."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"How a change in the library reaches each tool is described once, in the "
"s3Dgraphy manual: `Datamodel propagation "
"<https://docs.extendedmatrix.org/projects/s3dgraphy/en/v1.6/DATAMODEL_PROPAGATION.html>`__."
msgstr ""

msgid "The StratiGraph node"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **StratiGraph node** is the server of a project or an institution: a "
"laboratory, an excavation, a museum. It keeps the work that is shared."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Rooms.** A room is a study that lives on the node, where the members of"
" a project work together, each with a role (see :doc:`open-enter-"
"create`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The store of files.** The files of a room (photographs, models, "
"documents) are kept by the node, each under the fingerprint of its "
"content, with the licence and the embargo declared for it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The Catalog.** The studies the node publishes, fixed and citable, with "
"the reader page that shows a study's narrative to people who do not use "
"EMStudio."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Identity.** The node signs you in through ORCID and knows your role in "
"each room."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**EMStudio as a service.** The node serves EMStudio itself, at the "
"address ``/em/studio/`` of the node. The web version is this page: the "
"same program as the desktop application, with the node behind it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Plugins of the node.** Code that the node's administrator installed, "
"which the node runs for a room in a closed space (see :doc:`extensions-"
"and-trust`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The desktop application works with a node too: you sign in from the chip "
"at the top right of the window, and the rooms of the node are then one "
"click away. Without a node, EMStudio is complete on its own, on the files"
" of your disk."
msgstr ""

msgid "EM Tools in Blender"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**EM Tools** is the Blender add-on of the Extended Matrix: the place "
"where the proxies of the units are modelled, the representation models "
"are prepared and the reconstruction is built in 3D. EMStudio and EM Tools"
" work together in two ways."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Live.** With the mode **With an app** of the chip's menu, EMStudio and "
"Blender share the study as you work: selecting a unit in one selects it "
"in the other, and the changes made in Blender reach the graph (see :doc"
":`/how-to/extensions/live-sync-with-em-tools`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**By composing a file.** ``File ▸ Export ▸ Blender scene (.blend)…`` "
"builds a Blender file from the study, with the models and proxies the "
"graph names (see :doc:`/how-to/extensions/compose-a-blend`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EM Tools reads the colour schemes kept in the ``em.json``, and it uses "
"the same vocabularies and extensions installed on the computer."
msgstr ""

msgid "Heriverse"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Heriverse** is the platform that shows a study online and in immersive "
"reality. The **Archive** workspace prepares the versions of each model "
"for its use, among them *Heriverse / web* (see :doc:`/how-to/archive"
"/consolidate-for-a-target`). The section **Open with…** of the "
"**Inspector** opens a node's scene in Heriverse when a resource of the "
"study names one."
msgstr ""

msgid "Other sources"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Material from other programs enters the study through **Bring in…** "
"(files brought in as documents), through a table read with a mapping, or,"
" for Tropy, through **Import from Tropy…** in the menu of the **Shelf** "
"(see :doc:`/how-to/bring-in/index`). Whatever enters becomes a document "
"of the study, with its date and its provenance."
msgstr ""

msgid "Every act signed"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Whatever tool writes into a study, the act carries its author. An edit "
"made in EMStudio, a change sent from Blender, a command of a plugin, a "
"draft written with an AI: each is recorded with who made it and, when a "
"program made it, with the program as well. A study can always answer the "
"question *who said this?*"
msgstr ""

